   "content": "# How a Heat Pump Works\n\nA heat pump sounds like it should create heat, but it doesn't. It moves heat. That's the entire trick.\n\nThink of your refrigerator. It takes heat from inside the box and dumps it into the kitchen. That's why the back of a fridge is warm. A heat pump does the same thing, but in reverse. It takes heat from the outside air (or the ground) and dumps it into your home.\n\nThis works even when it's cold outside, which surprises a lot of people. The air at 20\u00b0F still contains heat energy. It's just spread out and at a low temperature. A heat pump can grab that energy and concentrate it.\n\nHere's the cycle step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Absorb heat.** A liquid refrigerant flows through a coil called the evaporator. This coil sits on the outdoor side of the system. The refrigerant inside the coil is very cold\u2014colder than the outside air. Because heat always flows from warm to cold, the outside air gives up some of its heat to the refrigerant. The refrigerant absorbs that heat and turns from a liquid into a low-pressure gas.\n\n**Step 2: Compress it.** A compressor, which is basically a pump, takes that low-pressure gas and squeezes it. This is the key step. When you compress a gas, its temperature shoots up. Think of how a bicycle pump gets warm when you pump it fast. The refrigerant gas, now compressed, can be much hotter than the air inside your house.\n\n**Step 3: Release the heat.** That hot, high-pressure gas flows through a second coil called the condenser, which sits on the indoor side. The gas is now hotter than your indoor air, so it gives its heat to your home. As it loses that heat, it condenses back into a liquid. Your living room gets warmer.\n\n**Step 4: Expand it.** The liquid refrigerant then passes through a small valve called the expansion valve. This valve restricts the flow, which causes the pressure and temperature to drop sharply. The refrigerant becomes cold again\u2014colder than the outside air. Now it's ready to go back to Step 1 and absorb more heat from outside.\n\nThe cycle repeats continuously.\n\n**Why is this efficient?**\n\nA gas furnace or electric heater has to create heat from scratch. A heat pump doesn't create heat. It just moves it. For every one unit of electrical energy it uses to run the compressor and fan, it can move two, three, or even four units of heat from outside into your home. That's why heat pumps can be 200\u2013400% efficient, while the best gas furnace tops out at around 98%.\n\n**What about very cold weather?**\n\nOn a mild day, the outside air has plenty of heat to harvest. On a bitterly cold day, there's less available heat, so the system works harder and its efficiency drops. Modern cold-climate heat pumps can still operate effectively down to about 13\u00b0F or lower, though they may need a backup heat source (like a small electric resistance heater) on the coldest days.\n\n**In short:** a heat pump is a heat mover, not a heat maker. It uses a small amount of electricity to run a cycle that pulls thermal energy from the outside world and delivers it indoors, making it one of the most efficient ways to heat a building.",
